 ('p', 'Under frozen transfer, SwinFusion++ had a significantly higher ROC-AUC than Swin-B in seven of the nine directions, by +0.049 to +0.258 (Holm-adjusted p from < 0.005 to 0.018), and than Video Swin-B in three, Celeb-DF v2 → FF++ C40 (+0.0754), Celeb-DF v2 → DeeperForensics-1.0 (+0.0630), and DeeperForensics-1.0 → FF++ C40 (+0.0840), all with Holm-adjusted p < 0.005. In these three directions, it was significantly better than both encoders and had the highest ROC-AUC of the three models. SwinFusion++ was significantly lower than an encoder in none of the 18 comparisons. This is not evidence of non-inferiority: the five negative point estimates were not significant, but the widest interval reached −0.092 (DeeperForensics-1.0 → Celeb-DF v2, against Video Swin-B). SwinFusion++ also had the highest point estimate in FF++ C23 → C40 and DeeperForensics-1.0 → FF++ C23 (heatmap), but its lead over the runner-up, Video Swin-B, was not significant in either direction (+0.0014; and +0.0451 [+0.0077, +0.0816], Holm-adjusted p = 0.152, shown with an open marker in the forest plot).'),
 ('p', 'The advantage was clearest when the models were trained on Celeb-DF v2 or DeeperForensics-1.0: from these sources, SwinFusion++ was significantly better than Swin-B in all six directions and than Video Swin-B in three. From FF++ C23, SwinFusion++ was statistically indistinguishable from both encoders on FF++ C40 and Celeb-DF v2, and the one significant difference was the largest of all 18: on DeeperForensics-1.0, Swin-B’s ROC-AUC fell to 0.5678 [0.5250, 0.6105], while the two models with a temporal stream kept 0.8261 (SwinFusion++, +0.258 over Swin-B) and 0.8421 (Video Swin-B). The same ordering appeared on the 60-video subset that shares no source lineage with the FF++ C23 training or validation data (seed means: Swin-B 0.5322, SwinFusion++ 0.7356, Video Swin-B 0.7874; no interval; Appendix J, Table J3). This pattern is consistent with temporal evidence transferring better than frame appearance in this direction. Swin-B and Video Swin-B also differ in pretraining and input handling, however, so the comparison does not isolate the cause.'),
 ('p', 'The significant differences were stable across seeds and analysis choices. Nine of the ten had the same sign in all nine pairings of individual seeds, and the tenth (Celeb-DF v2 → FF++ C40 against Video Swin-B) in eight of nine; all ten remained significant under the family grouping recorded before scoring and under one pooled family, and no DeeperForensics-1.0-target decision changed with 175 lineage clusters (Appendix J, Table J7). Two limits apply. Transfer lowered ROC-AUC for every model, to between 0.459 and 0.976, and from DeeperForensics-1.0 to Celeb-DF v2 all three models were near chance (0.4593 to 0.6001). The three significant differences from DeeperForensics-1.0 to FF++ C23 and C40 could not be checked meaningfully on a lineage-free subset, because only 20 such test videos exist (Table VI; per-seed values in Table J3). Under matched training, SwinFusion++ therefore ranked target videos significantly better than Swin-B in seven of the nine transfer directions and than Video Swin-B in three. Whether these differences can be attributed to TLBG-HCA, rather than to the combination of two encoders, is examined under RQ4 with the topology and capacity-matched controls.'),

 ('sub', 'Threshold-Dependent Metrics Under Transfer'),
 ('p', 'RQ1 also concerns accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score. Table X gives these metrics at the frozen source thresholds, and the ROC curves in the figure after it show where each frozen threshold fell on the target curve.'),
 ('table', 'X', 'T7', 'Threshold-Dependent Metrics Under Frozen Transfer, Fixed Recipe'),
 ('fig',) + FIG_ROC,
 ('p', 'At the frozen source thresholds, every model became conservative on the target data. Specificity stayed between 91.11% and 100.00%, while recall was below 64% in every direction except FF++ C23 → C40 (79.29% to 87.14%). From the Celeb-DF v2 and DeeperForensics-1.0 sources, no model flagged more than 60 test videos as fake, so accuracy in those directions mainly reflects the share of real videos. From DeeperForensics-1.0 to Celeb-DF v2, where 86.20% of the test videos are fake, accuracy was only 13.80% to 15.24%, close to the 13.80% obtained by calling every video real.'),
 ('p', 'SwinFusion++ did not have the highest accuracy, recall, or F1-score in any of the nine directions (point estimates; several margins were one or two videos). From FF++ C23, its high precision and specificity reflect that it made the fewest positive calls of the three models (114, 458, and 8 on C40, Celeb-DF v2, and DeeperForensics-1.0); from the other two sources, Swin-B made the fewest or as few as any other model. From FF++ C23 to Celeb-DF v2, for example, SwinFusion++ raised only six false alarms among 135 real videos (specificity 95.56%) but detected 53.62% of the fake videos, and its accuracy (59.41%) was lower than that of Swin-B (67.48%) and Video Swin-B (66.46%), although the three ROC-AUC values did not differ detectably. In the supplementary paired intervals (Table J2), its transfer accuracy was lower than an encoder’s in 6 of 18 comparisons (both encoders from FF++ C23 to C40 and to Celeb-DF v2; Video Swin-B from FF++ C23 to DeeperForensics-1.0 and from Celeb-DF v2 to C23) and higher in 2 (Swin-B from Celeb-DF v2 to C23 and to DeeperForensics-1.0).'),
 ('p', 'The ROC curves illustrate the split between ROC-AUC and accuracy. Outside FF++ C23 → C40, where ROC-AUC stayed near or above 0.8, the curves show that the ranking of fake above real videos was largely retained, but the frozen operating point sat in the low-recall part of the curve. Moving it would require target labels, which the frozen-transfer design excludes. Where ROC-AUC was near 0.5, as from DeeperForensics-1.0 to Celeb-DF v2, the ranking itself was close to chance, so the problem was not only the threshold. For all three models, the frozen screening label on an unseen dataset is therefore much less informative than the ranking results suggest.'),

